- op: discuss  # c1c0
  at: '2026-08-02T18:30:53Z'  # c1c0
  by: agent  # c1c0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c1c0
  clock: 3  # c1c0
  nonce: c1c0  # c1c0
  message: "GOVERNANCE APPROVAL REQUESTED 2026-08-02. Design complete, NOT implemented.\nTouches scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh, scripts/merge-pr and scripts/auto-pr - all\nthree are governance files, so per AGENTS.md no PR is opened until approved.\n\nCONSUMER AUDIT - the filing named one site; there are FOUR that fail open.\n\n  merge-pr:297        enumerates 1,2      -> falls through to merge on exit 3\n  auto-pr:2065        enumerates 1,2      -> falls through to merge on exit 3\n                                             (this is the POST-REBASE merge path)\n  auto-pr:1349        enumerates 3,1,2    -> falls through on any future code\n  auto-pr:1996        enumerates 3,1,2    -> falls through on any future code\n  forgejo-api:1401    proceeds only rc==0 -> SAFE\n  forgejo-api:1746    returns 0 only rc==0-> SAFE\n\nThe two safe sites are safe for exactly the reason L54 gives: they enumerate the\nPERMITTING case. The four unsafe ones enumerate the blocking cases.\n\nPROPOSED FIX - a chokepoint, not four patches.\n\nIntroduce one helper in forgejo-api.sh:\n\n    ci_verdict_permits_merge RC [PR_NUM]\n      returns 0 ONLY when RC is 0; for every other value (known or not) it\n      prints that code's existing recovery guidance and returns RC.\n      Unknown codes get: \"poll_ci returned an unrecognised verdict (RC) -\n      refusing to merge.\"\n\nEach of the four sites then reduces to:\n\n    poll_ci \"$HEAD_SHA\" || poll_result=$?\n    ci_verdict_permits_merge \"$poll_result\" \"$PR_NUM\" || exit $?\n\nThis gives the question \"may we merge on this verdict?\" ONE home instead of\nfour hand-rolled copies, makes default-deny structural rather than remembered,\nand is directly unit-testable by sourcing the library - no live forge needed,\nwhich is what kept the existing seam tests at the grep level. auto-pr's exit-2\nand exit-3 RETRY loops are untouched; they run before the final decision and\nstill need the raw code.\n\nWI-ninar, same PR (the two are one failure when combined):\npoll_ci decides \"has any job started\" by asking whether any status context has\ngone NON-pending. Woodpecker posts ONE aggregate context that stays pending for\nthe whole run, so that is false for the entire duration and every pipeline over\nCI_STALE_PENDING_SECONDS (300s default; our suite is ~360s) returns exit 3.\nFix is the filing's option (a): ask whether ANY context EXISTS for the SHA.\nWoodpecker posts it when the run is created, so its presence proves dispatch.\nRename any_job_started -> run_dispatched and reword the message to name what is\nactually being detected (\"no CI status context exists for <sha> after Ns - the\npipeline was never dispatched\"). This restores the original intent: catch a\nDISPATCH failure, not a slow run. Explicitly NOT raising\nCI_STALE_PENDING_SECONDS, which the filing rules out.\n\nWHY BOTH IN ONE PR: fixing only WI-ditav makes every pipeline over 300s refuse\nto merge (correct but useless); fixing only WI-ninar leaves the fail-open path\nlive for every other verdict. Neither is safe to ship alone.\n\nTESTS: behavioural, via the existing AUTOPR_TEST_POLL_EXITS seam - assert\nci_verdict_permits_merge returns non-zero for 1, 2, 3 AND for an unallocated\ncode (e.g. 7), and 0 only for 0. Plus a recurrence guard that fails if any\npoll_ci consumer regains an enumerate-the-blocking-codes shape.\n\nOPEN QUESTION FOR THE HUMAN, from the WI-ditav filing and still unanswered:\nwhat does GitHub branch protection on dev actually REQUIRE? It did not block\nthe pending-CI merge of PR #160. I cannot query it - no approved script exposes\nbranch-protection settings, and adding that to ci-debug is itself a governance\nchange. Needs either a human check of the repo settings or approval to extend\nci-debug."  # c1c0
